#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM8036", unit: "U3")
#let items = (
p(1,"E","U3.1 Typing judgments",[Give a derivation of $lambda x.x:A->A$ in the empty context.],[Start with the variable rule.],[Discharge $x:A$ by abstraction.],[The variable rule gives $x:A tack.r x:A$. The abstraction rule then gives $tack.r lambda x.x:A->A$. The premise explicitly contains the assumption discharged by the conclusion.],[Show the premise context and discharged assumption.]),
p(2,"E","U3.1 Application",[In context $f:A->B,x:A$, derive $f x:B$.],[Use the variable rule twice.],[Apply the arrow elimination rule.],[The context contains $f:A->B$ and $x:A$, giving both variable judgments. Arrow elimination yields $f x:B$ in the same context. No assumption is discharged.],[The argument type must be exactly the arrow domain.]),
p(3,"E","U3.1 Arrow association",[Give a type and derivation for $lambda x.lambda y.x$.],[Arrows associate right.],[First abstract over $y$, then $x$.],[From $x:A,y:B tack.r x:A$, abstract over $y$ to get $x:A tack.r lambda y.x:B->A$, then over $x$ to obtain $A->(B->A)$.],[Do not confuse this with $(A->B)->A$.]),
p(4,"E","U3.2 Substitution",[Given $x:A tack.r x:A$ and $tack.r u:A$, calculate the conclusion of substitution.],[Replace the free variable.],[The result type remains $A$ in simple typing.],[Substitution gives $tack.r x[x:=u]:A$, namely $tack.r u:A$. This smallest instance makes clear that the substituted assumption is removed from the context.],[State both the result term and resulting context.]),
p(5,"C","U3.1 Composition",[Derive the type of $lambda f.lambda g.lambda x.f(g x)$ from chosen types $f:B->C,g:A->B$.],[Work from the innermost application outward.],[Discharge assumptions in reverse order.],[In context $f:B->C,g:A->B,x:A$, obtain $g x:B$ and then $f(g x):C$. Three abstractions yield $(B->C)->(A->B)->A->C$, with right association throughout.],[Show the two intermediate application judgments.]),
p(6,"C","U3.2 Weakening",[Prove admissibility of adding a fresh unused variable $y:B$ to a simply typed context.],[Induct on the typing derivation.],[Rename abstraction binders to remain fresh for the new context.],[Induct on the original typing derivation. For a variable, its original declaration is still present. The constant rule is context-independent. For application, weaken both premises and apply the same rule. For abstraction, rename its bound variable away from $y$, weaken the body derivation under the extended context, and reapply abstraction. The resulting term and type are unchanged.],[Treat the binder case and freshness explicitly.]),
p(7,"C","U3.2 Preservation",[Prove preservation for a root redex $(lambda x.t)u -> t[x:=u]$ using inversion and substitution.],[Invert application, then abstraction.],[The body premise has the exact assumption required by substitution.],[If $Gamma tack.r (lambda x.t)u:B$, application inversion supplies some $A$ with $Gamma tack.r lambda x.t:A->B$ and $Gamma tack.r u:A$. Abstraction inversion supplies $Gamma,x:A tack.r t:B$. Substitution therefore gives $Gamma tack.r t[x:=u]:B$.],[Identify the same domain type in both premises.]),
p(8,"C","U3.2 Open terms and progress",[Give a well-typed open term that is neither a call-by-value value nor able to step, under the lecture convention that values are abstractions and the inert base constant.],[A neutral application has no visible function constructor.],[Use $f:o->o,x:o$.],[In context $f:o->o,x:o$, the term $f x:o$ is typable. It is not an abstraction or constant. Neither subterm steps, and $f$ is not an abstraction, so no call-by-value rule applies. The closedness assumption in progress prevents this example.],[Use the specified value convention and explain all possible step rules.]),
p(9,"C","U3.1 Untypability of self-application",[Prove $lambda x.x x$ is untypable in finite simple types.],[Invert the two uses of $x$ in one context.],[Its type would have to equal an arrow having itself as domain.],[Suppose the declaration of $x$ is $A$. Typing $x x$ requires a type $B$ with $A=A->B$: the function occurrence has type $A->B$, while the argument occurrence has type $A$. A finite simple type has a finite number of constructor nodes, whereas $A->B$ has strictly more nodes than $A$. Thus this equality is impossible.],[Explain why one variable cannot receive unrelated types in the same context.]),
p(10,"A","U3.2 Failure of subject expansion",[Construct a beta step from an untypable term to a typable closed term. Prove both typing assertions.],[An ignored argument may itself be untypable.],[Use $(lambda x.lambda y.y)Omega$.],[Take $(lambda x.lambda y.y)Omega$. It reduces to $lambda y.y$, typable at $A->A$. If the source were typable, application inversion would make $Omega=Delta Delta$ typable and hence would make $Delta=lambda z.z z$ typable. The finite-type argument of Exercise #link(label("MM8036-U3.09"))[U3.09] rules that out. Thus typing is preserved forward by reduction but need not be reflected backward.],[Use inversion to justify untypability of the whole source.]),
p(11,"A","U3.2 Substitution proof",[Prove the application and abstraction cases of the substitution lemma, including why a binder may need renaming.],[State the induction hypothesis for each immediate premise.],[For an abstraction, choose its binder fresh for the substituting term and distinct from the variable being removed.],[Assume $Gamma,x:A tack.r t:B$ and $Gamma tack.r u:A$. For an application $s r$, inversion gives $s:C->B$ and $r:C$ under the extended context; induction substitutes in both, and application yields $s[x:=u]r[x:=u]:B$. For $t=lambda y.s$, rename $y$ fresh. Its body has a derivation under $Gamma,x:A,y:C$. Weaken the typing of $u$ by $y:C$, apply induction to the body (using the harmless exchange of distinct simple declarations), then abstract over $y$. Capture avoidance ensures this abstraction is the intended substituted term.],[Track contexts and justify weakening rather than assuming the argument is typable under any context.]),
p(12,"A","U3.1 Typing restricts syntax",[Prove that a closed beta-normal arrow-only term of base type $o$, in the language with exactly one inert constant $c:o$, must be $c$.],[Classify normal terms into abstractions and neutral spines.],[A closed neutral spine must be headed by a constant.],[A normal term is an abstraction or a variable/constant followed by zero or more arguments: otherwise an application with an abstraction head would be a redex. A term of base type cannot be an abstraction. Closedness excludes a variable at the head of the outer spine. The only constant is $c:o$, which cannot accept an argument because its type is not an arrow. Hence the spine has no arguments and the term is $c$. Bound variables inside possible arguments do not change the absence of a suitable outer head.],[Use normality, closedness, and the constant's type separately.]),
)
